<section class="eh-section eh-faq">
  <div class="eh-section-label">faq</div>
  <details>
    <summary>Do I need to rebuild my analytics stack &mdash; warehouse, pipelines, dashboards?</summary>
    <p>No. Your warehouse and dashboards do exactly what they were designed for, and they stay. This is a complementary layer: it watches for events outside the predefined vocabulary, and when one proves to matter, it graduates into your existing governed layer &mdash; a new row in the warehouse, not a new warehouse.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Is this a new concept? Why now?</summary>
    <p>The roots are respectable &mdash; software observability and event sourcing have argued for years that systems should capture more than anyone predefines. What is new is applying that discipline to business analytics and BI strategy. And &ldquo;why now&rdquo; is simple arithmetic: the pace at which the world generates new kinds of events has outrun the pace at which schemas get updated. The gap between those two speeds is where this layer works.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>What will this tell me that my current reports don&rsquo;t?</summary>
    <p>Your reports answer questions about events someone already defined &mdash; and they should keep doing that. This layer surfaces a different class of information: events happening in and around the business that have no name yet, the early form of opportunities and threats. The deliverable is exactly that &mdash; unknown events captured, classified, and made known before they arrive as a surprise inside a KPI.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>How do we start?</summary>
    <p>With a short conversation about what your current reporting can&rsquo;t tell you &mdash; then typically a small, scoped pilot: one data stream, a few weeks, ending with a set of newly named events and a live dashboard you can interrogate yourself. See <a href="work-with-me.html">work with me</a>.</p>
  </details>
</section>

